  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1137 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nOn Thursday a large upper level trough will be located across the \nwestern CONUS. An embedded upper level shortwave trough will track \nnortheast across West Texas late Thursday into Friday. Meanwhile the \nremnant mid and upper level moisture from Tropical Storm Vance will \nmove north into the region. The combination of abundant low level \nmoisture in the Gulf along with the added upper level moisture from \nthe remnants of Vance will result in the potential for locally heavy \nrainfall across the area. The current forecast has medium to high \n(40-90%) rain chances across the area Thursday. Rain chances \nincrease even more Thursday night into Friday morning, with medium \nto high (60-100%) rain chances. The highest rain chances will be \nacross the western half of the area. The main question, as is \nusually the case, is where the heaviest rain ends up. Some of the \nguidance indicates the heaviest rain occurring across West Central \nTexas, especially the western half of the area. Other model guidance \nhas the axis of heaviest rain to our west, across the Trans-Pecos \nregion and Permian Basin. Ultimately, it will depend on the eventual \ntrack of the aforementioned upper level shortwave trough. We should \nhave a better idea in the next 24 hours as more high resolution \nmodel guidance begins to extend into that time frame. The main \nconcern continues to be locally heavy rainfall as precipitable water \nvalues increase to 1.6 to 1.9 inches. The locally heavy rainfall may \nresult in flooding. Highs on Thursday will be in the mid 70s to \nlower 80s, with highs on Friday in the low to mid 70s \n\nLow end (20-30%) rain chances are forecast on Saturday (Halloween). \nDrier conditions are then forecast for the evening hours. The next \nupper level trough will approach the forecast area Sunday into \nMonday. There are differences in the timing and strength of this \nfeature, so confidence remain low for this time frame. This upper \nlevel trough/low will bring the next chance of rain, especially \nMonday into Monday night. For now, rain chances were kept in the 20-\n30% range.",
